<h2 id="applications-heading">Gravity Roller Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Loading Docks and Truck Loading</h3>
<p>Gravity roller conveyors are the standard equipment at loading docks worldwide. Workers slide cases down a slight decline from the dock into the trailer. Flexible/expandable gravity roller sections telescope from 8 to 24 feet to reach deep into trailers without workers climbing inside. Skate wheel alternatives exist for very lightweight packages, but gravity rollers handle a wider weight range.</p>
<h3>Manual Packing and Shipping Stations</h3>
<p>Pack stations use gravity roller conveyors to queue empty cartons, move packed cartons to taping stations, and transport sealed cartons to shipping. These level (zero-decline) sections rely on manual push to move products. Workers appreciate the low friction and quiet operation. Gravity roller sections between workstations cost a fraction of <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> and require zero energy.</p>
<h3>End-of-Line Packaging</h3>
<p>Many <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line layouts</a> use gravity roller sections between case sealers and palletizing areas. The slight decline moves sealed cases from the case sealer to a staging area where palletizers or manual stackers pick them up. These sections typically connect to <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a> sections upstream.</p>
<h3>Assembly and Inspection</h3>
<p>Manufacturing facilities use gravity roller conveyors as assembly and inspection workstation lines. Products sit on the rollers while workers assemble, inspect, or rework them, then push the product to the next station. The open-roller design provides access to the product bottom. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyors</a> offer a powered alternative for higher-throughput assembly lines.</p>
<h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage operations</a> use <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary stainless steel gravity roller conveyors</a> for cased product in dry environments. The open-roller design allows debris to fall through rather than accumulating on a belt surface. Sealed stainless steel bearings and smooth-welded frames support washdown cleaning protocols.</p>
<h2 id="upgrade-heading">When to Choose Powered Over Gravity</h2>
<p>Gravity roller conveyors excel in specific scenarios but have clear limitations. Upgrade to a powered system when any of these conditions apply:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>You need controlled speed.</strong> Gravity conveyors deliver variable speed that depends on product weight and decline angle. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">Powered roller conveyors</a> maintain consistent speed regardless of load.</li>
<li><strong>You need uphill transport.</strong> Gravity works downhill only. Any incline or level transport over long distances requires a powered system.</li>
<li><strong>You need accumulation.</strong> Products on gravity conveyors pile up with full backpressure at the low end. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> and <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR systems</a> provide zero-pressure queuing.</li>
<li><strong>You need integration with automated systems.</strong> PLC-controlled packaging lines require conveyors that start, stop, and vary speed on command. <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">Automated conveyor systems</a> use powered drive technologies.</li>
<li><strong>Product weight varies significantly.</strong> A gravity decline tuned for 50 lb cases will over-accelerate 10 lb cases and stall 100 lb cases. Powered conveyors handle variable loads at consistent speed.</li>
<li><strong>You need direction changes.</strong> Gravity conveyors can navigate gentle curves but cannot execute 90-degree <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a> or multi-directional routing without powered assist.</li>
</ul>
<p>Gravity roller conveyors remain the right choice for simple point-to-point transport with consistent product weight, available decline, no accumulation requirement, and budget priority. They also serve as the standard gravity take-away section downstream of powered systems.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What angle do you need for a gravity roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Most gravity roller conveyors require a decline of 3 to 5 degrees (approximately 3/8" to 5/8" drop per foot). Heavier products need less decline. Lighter products need more. The correct pitch depends on product weight, roller bearing quality, and roller diameter. Test with your actual product before committing to a fixed installation angle.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the weight limit for a gravity roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Light-duty gravity rollers handle up to 50-75 lbs. Medium-duty handles 75-200 lbs. Heavy-duty gravity roller sections with 3.5" diameter rollers and structural frames handle 500+ lbs. For loads above 500 lbs, consider <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> for positive chain drive.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How much does gravity roller conveyor cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Gravity roller conveyor is the lowest cost type ($). Cost factors include frame material (aluminum, galvanized steel, or stainless steel), roller diameter and gauge, bearing type, overall width, and length. Stainless steel construction for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food-grade applications</a> costs significantly more. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for a full breakdown.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What roller spacing should I use?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>At least 3 rollers must always contact the product bottom. Divide your smallest product length by 3, round down to the nearest standard pitch (1.5", 2", 3", 4.5", or 6"). A 12-inch case needs 4-inch or closer spacing. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor hub page</a> for more details on pitch selection.</p>

</div>
</div>
